  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY NIGHT THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 1255 PM MDT Fri Jun 26 2026\n\nThere is decent agreement among the various models and ensembles\nthrough much of the extended period. The upper pattern will\nfavor a trough across the western states, with persistent dry\nsouthwest flow across Colorado into the middle of next week.\n\nSaturday night through Wednesday...persistent southwest flow\naloft will favor continued high fire danger across southern\nColorado. Humidity values will remain low, with values at or\nunder 10 percent each afternoon, with poor humidity recoveries\novernight. Winds will remain gusty each day, with southwest\nwinds 15-25 mph with gusts 30-40 mph each afternoon. No\nprecipitation is forecast during this period across southern\nColorado. Daytime highs will remain hot, with 90s to lower 100s\neach afternoon, with overnight lows mainly in the 50s across the\nlower elevations. A Fire Weather Watch is in place for Sunday\nmorning into the afternoon, mainly for the Mountains and San\nLuis Valley. The Plains continue to reach criteria as well, but\nas mentioned above, fuels are in question given recent spotty\nheavy rainfall. By Monday, we may need to rethink issuing fire\nweather highlights on the Plains as fuels begin to dry out.\n\nThursday into Friday...the upper pattern still favors southwest\nflow across southern Colorado, however, energy in more southerly\nflow may move northward across the Colorado-Kansas border. This\nmay lead in an uptick of showers and thunderstorms near the\nborder. There are model differences out this far in the\nforecast, and confidence is low in shower and thunderstorm\noccurrence at this time. Temperatures will remain hot, with 90s\nto lower 100s on the Plains.",
